From e36363f6e01bf0303b6d365af77f4d8babe56252 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: steamac <steamac@local>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 17:14:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] virtio-snd: keep CoreAudio control requests and the pump from stalling each other

Symptom: multi-second game freezes; the guest driver logs `control message (0x..)
timeout` (PCM_PREPARE/RELEASE) and then `SID 0: invalid I/O queue state` in a storm
(4905 lines, 180 s without frames in one run). The guest counts a playback message as
in flight until the device completes it and refuses the next PREPARE while any is, and
a control message that times out is never retired, so nothing recovers.

Upstream state (libkrun v1.19.6): the CoreAudio backend is steamac's own (patch 0014),
there is nothing upstream to configure. Cause, in this backend:

* prepare/start/release ran stop, flush, configure (AudioUnitInitialize) and
  AudioOutputUnitStart while holding `Inner::hosts`, which the pump thread takes every
  iteration; the pump could not complete queued I/O meanwhile. The virtio-snd device has
  a single worker thread for every queue, so a control request that blocked in an
  AudioUnit call (or on `hosts`) also stopped it popping the TX queue.
* capture stop (`stop_capture`) ran AudioOutputUnitStop under `hosts` as well.
* nothing completed messages when the pump stopped making progress, and nothing bounded
  a control request.
* follow_input_device restarted capture on every housekeeping tick while the start worker
  was still blocked (capture_device was only set after the blocking calls) and every
  START spawned a worker: 704 stuck "virtio-snd capture" threads in one run.

Changes (all in the CoreAudio backend):

* The playback unit lives in a `UnitSlot`; prepare/start/release take it out of the
  stream and run their AudioUnit calls on a short-lived thread (`unit_job`) without
  `hosts`. The pump sees an unconfigured stream meanwhile and carries on. The caller waits
  at most CTL_DEADLINE (2 s, below the guest's virtio_snd.msg_timeout_ms of 10 s) and
  otherwise answers IO_ERR instead of never; the job finishes in the background and a
  request meanwhile fails at once as busy. Control requests take `hosts` with a deadline
  too (`lock_hosts`).
* Capture stop detaches the unit under the lock and stops it outside.
* A watchdog thread fails every queued playback/capture message with IO_ERR once the pump
  has made no progress for IO_STALL (3 s; an idle pump wakes every second), so the guest's
  queues drain and its driver restarts the stream. In-flight playback messages moved into
  `Shared` so the watchdog can reach them without `hosts`.
* Capture start: one worker per stream, the newest request wins, a STOP drops an unserved
  request, and the attempted input device is recorded before the blocking AudioUnit calls
  (`StartGate`; from the lean agent's snd-capture-gate.diff).

Tests (cargo test --features gpu,snd --lib -- virtio::snd, now run by build.sh): the pump
completes I/O while a unit job is in flight; a stuck unit job fails in time, the next
fails at once as busy, and the stream recovers; control requests fail within the deadline
while the pump holds `hosts`; the watchdog fails queued I/O while the pump is stuck and
leaves an idle pump alone; three StartGate tests. The two stuck-pump tests, run against
the code before this patch, hang (control request) and fail (watchdog). The crate's
other snd test modules (stream.rs, audio_backends.rs) were written for the
vhost-user-device this code came from and do not compile in libkrun (no init_logger,
NullBackend, MockSplitQueue); they are disabled so the macOS backend's tests can run.
rustfmt (stable) was applied to coreaudio.rs.

Output routing (patch 0017): the unit slot carries the OutputRoute and the speaker mixer, so a
control request builds the device-bound HAL unit and its channel map off `hosts` like any other
unit; the pump re-routes through the same slot.

Limit: AudioUnit calls the pump thread makes itself (output-device switch, resume after
pause, latency refresh) still run under `hosts`; a hang there is covered by the watchdog
and the control deadlines, not prevented.

